        "id": "p33-q1",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain how you would find the sign of $a$, $b$ and $c$ without knowing explicitly what the equation of the parabola is. What did you find is a good order to find the coefficients? For example, is it best to find $c$ first, and then $b$, and then $a$? Explain why you think the order you have chosen is the best one.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "One useful order is $a$, $c$, then $b$. Concavity gives the sign of $a$; the $y$-intercept gives the sign of $c$. Use the vertex position and $x_V=-\\frac{b}{2a}$ to find the sign of $b$. Other justified orders are acceptable.",
          "worked": "First find $a$: an upward-opening parabola has $a>0$, and a downward-opening parabola has $a<0$.\n\nNext find $c$: the $y$-intercept is $(0,c)$, so its position above, on or below the $x$-axis gives $c>0$, $c=0$ or $c<0$.\n\nFinally use the vertex's horizontal position:\n$$\\begin{align*}x_V&=-\\frac{b}{2a}\\\\b&=-2ax_V.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $a>0$, a vertex left of the $y$-axis gives $b>0$, and a vertex right of it gives $b<0$. For $a<0$, these signs reverse. A vertex on the $y$-axis gives $b=0$.\n\nThis order is useful because concavity and the $y$-intercept can be read directly, and the sign of $a$ is then known when finding $b$. For the supplied graph, $a>0$, $c>0$ and $b>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 39
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q2",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Draw a number of parabolas with different positions and concavities. For each graph, state the sign of $a$, $b$, and $c$. Can any of the coefficients be zero? Explain your answer.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Examples: $y=x^2+2x+2$: $(+,+,+)$; $y=x^2-2x-1$: $(+,-,-)$; $y=-x^2+2x+1$: $(-,+,+)$; $y=-x^2$: $(-,0,0)$. Signs are listed in the order $(a,b,c)$. Either $b$ or $c$ can be zero; $a$ cannot be zero for a parabola.",
          "worked": "Possible sketches are shown below. Their features follow from:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=x^2+2x+2\\\\y&=(x+1)^2+1;\\end{align*}$$\nthis opens upwards, has vertex $(-1,1)$ and has $(a,b,c)=(1,2,2)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=x^2-2x-1\\\\y&=(x-1)^2-2;\\end{align*}$$\nthis opens upwards, has vertex $(1,-2)$ and has $(a,b,c)=(1,-2,-1)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=-x^2+2x+1\\\\y&=-(x-1)^2+2;\\end{align*}$$\nthis opens downwards, has vertex $(1,2)$ and has $(a,b,c)=(-1,2,1)$.\n\nThe graph $y=-x^2$ opens downwards with vertex at the origin and has $(a,b,c)=(-1,0,0)$.\n\nThe coefficient $b$ is zero when the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis. The coefficient $c$ is zero when the graph passes through the origin. Both can be zero together. The coefficient $a$ must be nonzero; otherwise the equation is not quadratic and its graph is not a parabola.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p33-q2-example-sketches",
              "role": "solution",
              "format": "tikz",
              "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source PDF page 33, p33-q2 authored example sketches\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.65cm,y=0.65cm,>=stealth]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\path[use as bounding box] (-3.2,-10.7) rectangle (11,5.5);\n\\begin{scope}\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (-3,0)--(2,0) node[right] {$x$};\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,-0.5)--(0,5) node[right] {$y$};\n\\draw[<->,line width=0.8pt,domain=-2.9:0.9,samples=70] plot (\\x,{(\\x+1)^2+1});\n\\node at (-0.5,-1.3) {$y=x^2+2x+2$};\n\\end{scope}\n\\begin{scope}[xshift=5.3cm]\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (-1.5,0)--(3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,-2.6)--(0,4) node[right] {$y$};\n\\draw[<->,line width=0.8pt,domain=-1.35:3.35,samples=70] plot (\\x,{(\\x-1)^2-2});\n\\node at (1,-3.4) {$y=x^2-2x-1$};\n\\end{scope}\n\\begin{scope}[yshift=-4.5cm]\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (-1.6,0)--(3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,-3.4)--(0,2.8) node[right] {$y$};\n\\draw[<->,line width=0.8pt,domain=-1.25:3.25,samples=70] plot (\\x,{-(\\x-1)^2+2});\n\\node at (1,-4.2) {$y=-x^2+2x+1$};\n\\end{scope}\n\\begin{scope}[xshift=5.3cm,yshift=-4.5cm]\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (-2.5,0)--(2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,-4.5)--(0,1) node[right] {$y$};\n\\draw[<->,line width=0.8pt,domain=-2:2,samples=70] plot (\\x,{-(\\x)^2});\n\\node at (0,-5.2) {$y=-x^2$};\n\\end{scope}\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
              "src": null,
              "widthMm": 150,
              "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
              "overlayOf": null,
              "transparent": false,
