        "answer": {
          "short": "Answers vary. The supplied signs $(+,+,-)$ are satisfied by $y=x^2+x-2$. Further examples are $(+,0,+)$ with $y=x^2+1$, and $(-,-,0)$ with $y=-x^2-x$. A choice $a=0$ cannot produce a parabola.",
          "worked": "For the supplied row, choose $a=1$, $b=1$ and $c=-2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=x^2+x-2\\\\y&=(x+2)(x-1)\\\\y&=\\left(x+\\frac12\\right)^2-\\frac94.\\end{align*}$$\nSketch an upward-opening parabola with $x$-intercepts $(-2,0)$ and $(1,0)$, $y$-intercept $(0,-2)$ and vertex $\\left(-\\frac12,-\\frac94\\right)$.\n\nFor a repeat, choose the signs Positive, Zero, Positive. The equation $y=x^2+1$ gives an upward-opening parabola with vertex $(0,1)$.\n\nFor another repeat, choose Negative, Negative, Zero:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=-x^2-x\\\\y&=-x(x+1)\\\\y&=-\\left(x+\\frac12\\right)^2+\\frac14.\\end{align*}$$\nThis parabola opens downwards, crosses the $x$-axis at $(-1,0)$ and $(0,0)$, and has vertex $\\left(-\\frac12,\\frac14\\right)$.\n\nIf the friend chooses Zero for $a$, explain that no parabola is possible because the quadratic term disappears.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p33-q3-example-sketches",
